findings.body:
The new helper discards replies only when `count % frequency != 0`. The original loops discarded replies only at multiples of 10000 (`== 0`). With the inverted check, the helper calls `$rd read` 10000 times on almost every iteration, including the first one. The deferring client has no pending replies on those calls, so each `read` blocks until the test times out.

The defrag suite uses this helper for every bulk-write loop, so most of the memefficiency tests will hang or fail. The refactor also changes the behavior of the existing call sites that rely on the helper's gating.

::diff{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="70-76"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The fix is one character. Change `!=` to `==`. The original inline loops reset the counter on exact multiples, and the helper should do the same.

::suggestion{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="71-71"}
        if {$count % $frequency == 0} {

`rdbLoadEmptyDbFunc` saves the previous value in `orig_active_defrag`, sets `server.active_defrag_enabled` to 0, runs the flush, and then sets it to 1. The saved value is never read. A replica configured with `activedefrag no` will therefore start running active defrag after its first full sync, even though the operator disabled it. This is a silent config change.

The same restore also turns defrag on in builds without HAVE_DEFRAG, where `activedefrag` cannot be enabled. The `INFO` output and `CONFIG GET` then report a state the build cannot honor.

The unused variable also triggers `-Wunused-but-set-variable` under the project's warning flags.

::diff{file="src/replication.c" lines="1956-1962"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The disable-during-flush idea is correct. The bug is only in the restore step.

::suggestion{file="src/replication.c" lines="1962-1962"}
    server.active_defrag_enabled = orig_active_defrag;

The new guard `if (!s->cgroups_ref) return 1;` returns "referenced" when there is no reference index. The index is NULL whenever no consumer group has any PEL entries, for example after the last XACK or after a reload with empty PELs. In that state no message is pending, so the correct return value is 0.

With this guard, ACKED trimming keeps every entry in such a stream. The trim loop in `streamTrim` (around line 823) keeps going until `s->length <= maxlen`, and only entries that are not referenced are removed. An acknowledged entry is never removed in this state, so ACKED-based trimming stops working. The stream grows without bound under `XADD ... MAXLEN ... ACKED`.

The new test in tests/unit/type/stream.tcl expects `XLEN 2` after the last XADD. Under the correct semantics, 1-0 (acked, PEL empty) and 2-0 (never delivered) should both be trimmed, leaving only 3-0, so `XLEN` should be 1. The assertion appears to lock in the buggy behavior, and its comment contradicts it. I have not run the test, so please verify this by running it.

::diff{file="src/t_stream.c" lines="2705-2712"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The crash is a NULL dereference in `raxFind`. The right fix is to return 0 from the NULL branch, not 1.

::suggestion{file="src/t_stream.c" lines="2708-2708"}
    if (!s->cgroups_ref) return 0;

The test at line 945 wraps all its assertions in `if {[$replica config get activedefrag] eq "activedefrag yes"}`, after a `catch` around `config set activedefrag yes`. On a build without jemalloc or without HAVE_DEFRAG, the set fails, the `if` is false, and the test passes with zero assertions. The other defrag tests in this file guard with `string match {*jemalloc*}` and `arenas.page`, and then fail explicitly when defrag cannot run.

The test also never checks the one thing it is meant to verify on the replica path, which is that defrag was not running during the flush. Its only assertion is `dbsize 0` after sync. A regression of the flush fix would not be caught on builds that skip the test silently.

::code{file="tests/unit/memefficiency.tcl" lines="979-1003"}

::callout{type="info"}
Use the same jemalloc guard as `test_active_defrag`, and have the test fail when the precondition is not met. Otherwise a skipped environment looks like a passing one.

Two entries use `#Q6601` and `#Q6621` instead of the `RediSearch/RediSearch#6601` form the PR description uses. The existing `#Q` entries later in the file (for example `#Q6430`) use a Query-engine prefix that does not match these references.

The new section also adds a `#14240` bullet for `INFO KEYSIZES`, which is not listed in the PR description. Confirm it belongs in 8.2.1 before merge.

